AI governance policies, standards, and procedures
The client's current AI governance policies, standards, and procedures, along with any charter documents.
    • Organizational structure / RACI for AI governance and AI security stakeholders (security architecture, GRC, platform/security operations, API security, AI/ML domain, and any agentic identity and access management program)
    • Any existing AI risk register or AI use-case inventory
Parent-organization AI governance and security policy
Where the client sits under a larger corporate parent, the higher-level governance and security policy documents that the client's own AI security standard must align to.
    • Any existing mapping between parent-level policy and the client's own standards
Existing AI security tooling and guardrails inventory
An inventory of AI security tooling currently deployed.
    • Gateways, data-loss-prevention/redaction tooling, monitoring, and guardrail products currently in use
    • Any system access documentation needed to review these tools directly
Phase 2 — Assessment and Refinement of AI Security Architecture Patterns
Currently drafted AI security architecture patterns
Any AI security architecture patterns the client has already drafted internally, ahead of the engagement.
    • Supporting reference architecture diagrams, control lists, or scope-of-applicability notes for each pattern
Agentic AI and MCP usage documentation
Documentation on the client's current or planned agentic AI and Model Context Protocol (MCP) usage.
    • MCP server inventory, if one exists (owners, deployment locations, tool exposure)
    • Any existing MCP proxy or gateway architecture documentation
Third-party LLM access documentation
Architecture and access model for third-party LLM services the client consumes (for example, a cloud provider's managed model service).
    • Credential and key management approach for these services
    • List of business units or applications currently consuming third-party LLM access
AI gateway architecture and technology documentation
Current or planned AI gateway architecture documentation.
    • Any AI gateway vendor(s) already evaluated or deployed
    • Gateway capability or requirements documentation, if one already exists
MCP proxy architecture documentation
MCP proxy patterns in use or planned, treated as distinct from the general AI/LLM gateway.
    • Any documentation distinguishing MCP-specific proxy controls from general LLM gateway controls
AI-powered IDE / agentic development environment usage
Which AI-powered IDEs or agentic development environments are approved or in use by developers.
    • Any governance policy or configuration standard already applied to these tools
    • Autonomy-mode defaults and approval-workflow configuration, if defined
Agentic AI embedded in third-party SaaS platforms
Documentation on agentic AI features enabled inside SaaS platforms the client already runs (for example, an embedded analytics or CRM agent).
    • Confirmation of deployment model per platform — this can materially change which vendor trust-layer protections actually apply
    • Current platform-level AI feature toggles or configuration
Customer-facing AI "chatbot" documentation
Architecture, scope, and controls for any customer-facing generative AI or chatbot deployment(s).
    • Whether the chatbot can take actions, versus answer-only, and what those actions are
    • Existing red-team or security testing results for the chatbot, if any
Internal AI assistant usage
Deployment and configuration documentation for internal AI assistants used by employees.
    • Identity and access model, and data-source connections, configured for each assistant
    • Any existing access-control audit results for retrieval-augmented internal assistants
Agentic identity and access management (IAM) program documentation
Where the client runs a separate program focused on agentic IAM, documentation to support coordination between that program and this engagement's pattern recommendations.
Security architecture patterns from any parallel engagement
If the client is running a related security architecture workstream with a different vendor or team in parallel, any patterns already produced there, to avoid duplicated work.
Phase 3 — AI Security Technical Standard
Existing draft AI security standard
The client's current draft of its AI security standard, where one already exists.
    • Any prior stakeholder feedback or socialization notes on the draft
AI security exception process documentation
The client's current exception process for AI security standard deviations, if one exists.
    • Historical exceptions granted, if tracked, as a reference point for process maturity
Phase 4 — AI and MCP Gateway Technology Analysis
Current AI/MCP gateway vendor landscape and tooling in use
Any AI or MCP gateway technology currently deployed or piloted.
    • Prior vendor evaluations, RFPs, or scoring exercises for gateway technology, if any exist
Existing agentic AI / API security platform configuration
Where the client already runs a platform providing AI-agent and API discovery/protection, its current configuration related to agentic AI detection and protection.
    • Confirmation of the licensed feature tier — specifically whether posture-management and detection/response capabilities for agentic AI are included, or only a legacy API-security tier
    • Network placement — perimeter/edge visibility only, versus visibility into internal (east-west) agent-to-API traffic
    • Current MCP-server discovery coverage within the platform, if any
Existing AI/MCP gateway technology selection criteria
Any prior selection criteria or scoring framework used for AI/MCP gateway technology, to be refined rather than built from scratch.
